  "worked_solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)=\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\u27e80,1\u27e9-\u27e81,0\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,1\u27e9 \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{\u2016\\overset{\\to }{AB}\u2016} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}\u27e8-1,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\u27e8-1,1\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\,f(x,y) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}y)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8{e}^{y}+2xy,\\,x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207\\,f(1,0) & =\u27e8{e}^{0}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 0,\\,1\\cdot {e}^{0}+{1}^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e81,\\,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(1,0) & =\u2207f(1,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =\u27e81,\\,2\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
